                <pb facs="0326" n="318" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg318"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1115/688: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-09-10T17:00:00+08:00">September 10, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 10—10:21 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>636. Department’s 222, September 8, 6 p.m. Reactions of Embassies to proposed
                    international train from Hankow to Canton are as follows: British generally
                    against evacuation, Hankow Consul General has full powers. French have
                    practically no nationals wishing to evacuate and are not interested. Italian and
                    German will cooperate.</p>
                <p>2. My 607 [<hi rendition="#italic">626?</hi>], September 8, 6 p.m.,<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d346-f1"/> was addressed to Hankow for action toward obtaining
                    definite information regarding number of American citizens willing to evacuate
                    by train.</p>
                <p>Department’s September 8, 6 p.m., and this reply have been repeated to Hankow,
                    Peiping and Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
